---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Header from '../../components/Header.astro';
import Footer from '../../components/Footer.astro';
import { specDrivenArticle as article } from '../../data/writing';
---
<BaseLayout title={`${article.title} | Haseeb Sultan`} description={article.description} article={{ published: article.published, image: article.image, headline: article.title }}>
  <Header />
  <main id="main" class="shell article-page">
    <article aria-labelledby="article-title">
      <figure class="article-cover">
        <img src={article.image} alt="Spec-Driven Development: Engineering mature software with AI coding agents. Tangled orange lines resolve into a structured architectural blueprint." width="2296" height="1196" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" />
      </figure>
      <header class="article-heading reading-column">
        <a href="/writing/" class="article-back">← All writing</a>
        <p class="eyebrow">{article.category}</p>
        <h1 id="article-title">{article.title}</h1>
        <p class="article-deck">{article.description}</p>
        <p class="article-byline">Haseeb Sultan <span aria-hidden="true">·</span> <time datetime={article.published}>September 20, 2026</time></p>
      </header>
      <div class="article-body reading-column">
        <nav class="article-contents" aria-label="In this article">
          <p>In this article</p>
          <ol>
            <li><a href="#foundations">Foundations of Spec-Driven Development</a></li>
            <li><a href="#benefits">Core benefits and strategic value</a></li>
            <li><a href="#governance">Project governance and the constitution</a></li>
            <li><a href="#lifecycle">The SDD feature development lifecycle</a></li>
            <li><a href="#strategies">Project types and application strategies</a></li>
            <li><a href="#tools">Tools for Spec-Driven Development</a></li>
          </ol>
        </nav>

        <section aria-labelledby="foundations">
          <h2 id="foundations">Foundations of Spec-Driven Development</h2>

          <h3>What is Spec-Driven Development?</h3>
          <p>In simple terms, <strong>Spec-Driven Development (SDD)</strong> means creating a clear plan or blueprint, called a specification, before asking an AI coding tool to write code.</p>
          <p>Instead of typing a quick request and hoping the AI guesses correctly, you write down:</p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>What</strong> you want to build.</li>
            <li><strong>Why</strong> you are building it.</li>
            <li><strong>The rules and constraints</strong> the AI must follow, including security requirements and technology choices.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>The AI reads this blueprint and uses it to guide the implementation, testing, and delivery of the software.</p>

          <h3>Vibe coding versus SDD</h3>
          <p><strong>Vibe coding</strong> works through trial and error. You give the AI a short prompt, such as “Make a login button,” inspect what it generates, and continue correcting it through a long conversation. This can be useful for a small experiment. As the system grows, however, earlier rules become easier to lose among new prompts, logs, and corrections.</p>

          <figure class="article-figure">
            <img src="/images/writing/spec-driven-development/vibe-coding-limits.png" alt="Diagram showing a tangled prompt, inspect, correct, and repeat loop that loses project decisions as a system grows." width="1520" height="883" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
            <figcaption>Iterative prompting works for isolated details, but conversations become a fragile place to store system-wide decisions.</figcaption>
          </figure>

          <p><strong>Spec-Driven Development</strong> moves project rules into durable documentation. The agent can re-read the specification before each task, keeping implementation decisions anchored to the same intent even when conversations and tools change.</p>

          <h3>Separate specification from implementation</h3>
          <p>This means keeping <strong>what and why</strong> separate from <strong>how</strong>. A specification might say, “Users need to save articles to read later.” The implementation decides whether those articles live in SQL, MongoDB, or browser storage.</p>
          <p>This separation makes change easier to reason about. If the storage technology changes, the product requirement remains stable while the technical plan identifies the affected code, schemas, handlers, tests, and deployment steps. The specification guides the change; review and validation confirm that the implementation still satisfies the intended behavior.</p>

          <h3>The architect and builder model</h3>
          <p>Think of building a house. The human acts as the architect: defining the blueprint, choosing the rooms, setting safety rules, and approving the result. The AI coding agent acts as the builder: creating plans, writing code, refactoring, and running tests within those boundaries.</p>

          <figure class="article-figure">
            <img src="/images/writing/spec-driven-development/human-architect-ai-builder.png" alt="Blueprint diagram showing the human architect defining intent and constraints while an AI coding agent implements, refactors, and tests from the specification." width="1520" height="805" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
            <figcaption>The developer owns intent and judgment; the agent executes against a persistent, reviewable specification.</figcaption>
          </figure>

          <p>You do not need to tell a professional builder how to hold a hammer. You provide a clear blueprint and inspect the work. In the same way, the AI supplies execution capacity while the specification carries the project’s intent.</p>
        </section>

        <section aria-labelledby="benefits">
          <h2 id="benefits">Core Benefits and Strategic Value</h2>

          <h3>High-leverage code control</h3>
          <p>A small change to a specification can coordinate a large implementation update. Imagine changing the approved database from SQLite to MongoDB. The revised specification gives the coding agent one reviewed source of intent from which to identify changes across schemas, persistence code, API handlers, tests, and deployment configuration.</p>
          <p>The agent still needs a migration plan, acceptance criteria, and human review. The leverage comes from directing related changes through one clear decision instead of manually rediscovering every dependency.</p>

          <h3>Eliminating context decay and drift</h3>
          <p>AI conversations have limited context. During a long session, early architecture rules can be displaced by error messages, generated code, design debates, and new requests. This is <strong>context decay</strong>. Once the agent starts making choices that conflict with the original design, it becomes <strong>context drift</strong>.</p>

          <figure class="article-figure">
            <img src="/images/writing/spec-driven-development/context-decay.png" alt="A funnel-shaped context window filled with prompts, errors, stack traces, and debates while foundational rules fall out through the bottom." width="1520" height="974" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
            <figcaption>Durable repository files keep architectural rules available when temporary conversation context becomes crowded.</figcaption>
          </figure>

          <p>SDD keeps essential project knowledge in version-controlled files rather than relying on a temporary chat window. Each task can begin by re-anchoring the agent to those files.</p>

          <h3>Maximizing intent fidelity</h3>
          <p><strong>Intent fidelity</strong> describes how closely the finished software matches the original goal. A vague prompt forces the AI to guess about architecture, user flows, interfaces, and failure handling. A specification makes those expectations explicit through requirements, acceptance criteria, and edge cases.</p>

          <h3>Mitigating technical debt</h3>
          <p>Technical debt grows when weak architectural choices, missing tests, and ignored edge cases accumulate. Because AI can produce code quickly, unclear direction can multiply these problems just as quickly. SDD introduces architectural guardrails, reviewable decisions, and validation checks before changes are accepted.</p>
        </section>

        <section aria-labelledby="governance">
          <h2 id="governance">Project Governance and the Constitution</h2>

          <h3>The project constitution</h3>
          <p>The <strong>Project Constitution</strong> is the master blueprint for the software. It is a central, version-controlled document that records the project’s durable rules before features are implemented.</p>
          <p>Because it lives in the repository as a standard document, the constitution can remain useful across tools. Whether the team uses GitHub Copilot, Claude Code, or another AI coding agent, each agent can work from the same project foundation.</p>

          <figure class="article-figure">
            <img src="/images/writing/spec-driven-development/project-constitution.png" alt="Architectural diagram showing a project supported by three constitution pillars: mission, technology stack, and roadmap." width="1520" height="818" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
            <figcaption>The constitution creates a stable center for product purpose, technical choices, and delivery direction.</figcaption>
          </figure>

          <h3>The three core pillars</h3>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Mission: Why does this project exist?</strong> Define the product’s goal, target users, problem, scope, and non-negotiable principles. The mission becomes a decision filter when a proposed feature adds complexity without helping the intended user.</li>
            <li><strong>Tech stack: What tools will we use?</strong> Record the approved languages, frameworks, databases, test tools, and deployment rules. This prevents an agent from choosing a different library or architecture for every task.</li>
            <li><strong>Roadmap: What are we building over time?</strong> Organize delivery into phases and milestones. The roadmap is a living document that keeps feature work focused while the project evolves.</li>
          </ul>

          <h3>Constitution versus AGENTS.md</h3>
          <p>Think of the constitution as the master blueprint for humans and AI. It explains the product purpose, technical direction, and roadmap at a high level.</p>
          <p><code>AGENTS.md</code> is the worker’s field guide for AI agents operating inside the repository. It contains practical instructions such as which commands to run, where assets belong, and which formatting or testing conventions to follow. The constitution tells the team what governs the project; <code>AGENTS.md</code> tells the agent how to work within it.</p>
        </section>

        <section aria-labelledby="lifecycle">
          <h2 id="lifecycle">The SDD Feature Development Lifecycle</h2>
          <p>Once the project rules are established, each feature moves through a continuous six-step loop:</p>

          <figure class="article-figure">
            <img src="/images/writing/spec-driven-development/sdd-iterative-engine.png" alt="Circular Spec-Driven Development lifecycle showing specify, plan, implement, validate, and replan around the project constitution." width="1520" height="820" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
            <figcaption>Specifications evolve with validated learning; they are active engineering tools rather than documents written once and forgotten.</figcaption>
          </figure>

          <h3>1. Specify: What and why</h3>
          <p>Describe what the user experiences and what success looks like without deciding every implementation detail. Define user flows, exact acceptance criteria, and edge cases such as a failed login or a lost network connection.</p>

          <h3>2. Plan: The technical blueprint</h3>
          <p>Choose the technical approach before writing code. The plan records the database schema, frameworks, API structures, security rules, dependencies, and other boundaries the implementation must respect.</p>

          <h3>3. Tasks: Bite-sized action items</h3>
          <p>Break the plan into small, testable pieces. Instead of asking the agent to “build the whole user system,” define focused tasks such as “Create the email validation endpoint” and “Add the password hash check.”</p>

          <h3>4. Implement: The AI writes the code</h3>
          <p>The coding agent completes one task at a time. Because each task traces back to a specification and plan, the generated code stays grounded in agreed requirements.</p>

          <h3>5. Validate and converge: The quality check</h3>
          <p>Compiling is not enough. Run automated tests, type checks, linting, reviews, and relevant operational checks until the implementation meets its acceptance criteria and reaches a verified state.</p>

          <h3>6. Replan: Learn and improve</h3>
          <p>Development is iterative. Update the roadmap, refine future specifications, and improve templates when implementation reveals new information. This closes the loop and gives the next feature a stronger starting point.</p>
        </section>

        <section aria-labelledby="strategies">
          <h2 id="strategies">Project Types and Application Strategies</h2>

          <h3>Match the specification to the project</h3>
          <p>Not every project needs the same level of rigor. A prototype may use a specification mainly to establish initial clarity. A production system benefits from specifications that evolve with the code and connect to automated tests. A highly mature platform may treat the specification as the source that triggers implementation changes.</p>

          <figure class="article-figure">
            <img src="/images/writing/spec-driven-development/specification-spectrum.png" alt="Three levels of specification maturity: spec-first for prototypes, spec-anchored for production teams, and spec-as-source for highly automated systems." width="1520" height="825" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
            <figcaption>Use enough specification discipline to control the project’s risk and complexity without creating unnecessary process.</figcaption>
          </figure>

          <p>SDD can then be applied according to the starting condition of the software: greenfield, brownfield, or legacy modernization.</p>

          <figure class="article-figure">
            <img src="/images/writing/spec-driven-development/greenfield-brownfield.png" alt="Split blueprint comparing a greenfield project built from a new constitution with a brownfield system analyzed to recover its current constraints." width="1520" height="822" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
            <figcaption>New systems begin with an explicit foundation; existing systems begin by discovering the architecture already in place.</figcaption>
          </figure>

          <h3>Greenfield projects: Building from scratch</h3>
          <p>A greenfield project is like building a new house on an empty plot. There is no existing code to constrain the design. The human and AI create the Project Constitution together, defining the mission, technical choices, and roadmap before beginning the first feature loop.</p>

          <h3>Brownfield projects: Adding to an existing house</h3>
          <p>A brownfield project already has an established codebase. The agent first inspects the folder structure, dependencies, interfaces, tests, and recurring patterns. The new specification must reflect the system’s real constraints so each feature feels native to the product rather than awkwardly attached.</p>

          <h3>Legacy modernization: Replacing old plumbing and wiring</h3>
          <p>A legacy system may contain valuable business logic inside code that is difficult to maintain. Instead of reproducing every old implementation detail, the team extracts the essential behavior into clear specifications. Those specifications guide a modern implementation while tests and staged migration protect the behavior users still depend on.</p>

          <p>Across all three project types, the central idea remains the same: preserve intent in a durable form, give the agent clear boundaries, and validate the result against the specification. That turns AI-assisted development from a sequence of isolated prompts into an engineering process the team can inspect, repeat, and improve.</p>
        </section>

        <section aria-labelledby="tools">
          <h2 id="tools">Tools for Spec-Driven Development</h2>

          <h3>1. GitHub Spec Kit</h3>
          <p><strong>What it is:</strong> An open-source toolkit created by GitHub to help developers establish structured, agent-guided engineering processes.</p>
          <p><strong>How it works:</strong> It provides a Python-based CLI called <code>specify-cli</code>. The CLI installs reusable agent skills for supported AI coding assistants, including GitHub Copilot, Claude Code, and Gemini CLI.</p>
          <p><strong>Key workflows:</strong></p>
          <ol>
            <li><strong>Spec-Driven Development:</strong> Uses commands in the AI chat, moving from <code>/speckit-constitution</code> to <code>/speckit-specify</code>, <code>/speckit-plan</code>, <code>/speckit-tasks</code>, <code>/speckit-implement</code>, and <code>/speckit-converge</code>.</li>
            <li><strong>Bug fixing:</strong> Uses the optional bug extension with <code>/speckit-bug-assess</code>, <code>/speckit-bug-fix</code>, and <code>/speckit-bug-test</code> to diagnose, repair, and verify defects separately.</li>
            <li><strong>Idea assessment:</strong> Uses the optional assessment extension, beginning with <code>/speckit-assess-intake</code> and ending with <code>/speckit-assess-decide</code>, to evaluate an idea before implementation.</li>
          </ol>
          <p><strong>Why it is useful:</strong> Spec Kit provides structured quality gates, including the <code>/speckit-converge</code> step, and repeatable processes that keep agents aligned with team rules, technical choices, and acceptance criteria.</p>
          <p class="repository-link"><a href="https://github.com/github/spec-kit" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">https://github.com/github/spec-kit</a></p>

          <h3>2. OpenSpec</h3>
          <p><strong>What it is:</strong> A lightweight SDD framework developed by Fission-AI for rapid iteration by individual developers and larger teams.</p>
          <p><strong>How it works:</strong> Install it with Node.js using <code>npm install -g @fission-ai/openspec@latest</code>. It supports more than 30 AI coding assistants, including Cursor, GitHub Copilot, Amazon Q, and Codex. Command syntax can vary by assistant.</p>
          <p><strong>Key workflows:</strong></p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Interactive commands:</strong> <code>/opsx:explore</code> helps investigate an idea, <code>/opsx:propose</code> creates structured change artifacts, <code>/opsx:apply</code> carries out the implementation tasks, and <code>/opsx:archive</code> records the completed change.</li>
            <li><strong>Folder-based artifacts:</strong> Each feature change has its own folder with <code>proposal.md</code>, <code>specs/</code> for requirements and scenarios, <code>design.md</code>, and <code>tasks.md</code>.</li>
            <li><strong>Stores:</strong> This beta feature allows teams to keep planning specifications in a dedicated repository shared across frontend, backend, and library repositories.</li>
          </ul>
          <p><strong>Why it is useful:</strong> OpenSpec is fluid and less rigid. Teams can update artifacts as they learn, making it easy to introduce on existing brownfield projects.</p>
          <p class="repository-link"><a href="https://github.com/Fission-AI/OpenSpec" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">https://github.com/Fission-AI/OpenSpec</a></p>

          <h3>Quick comparison: Which one should you use?</h3>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Choose Spec Kit</strong> if you want a highly structured, opinionated workflow with explicit validation through <code>converge</code> and dedicated processes for bug fixing and idea assessment.</li>
            <li><strong>Choose OpenSpec</strong> if you want a lightweight, flexible framework that supports cross-repository planning through Stores and allows rapid iteration with less ceremony.</li>
          </ul>
        </section>
        <a href="/writing/" class="text-link">Explore more writing →</a>
      </div>
    </article>
  </main>
  <Footer />
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .article-page { padding-block: 40px 76px; }
  .article-cover img { display: block; width: 100%; height: auto; border: 1px solid var(--color-line); border-radius: 8px; }
  .reading-column { max-width: 760px; margin-inline: auto; }
  .article-heading { padding-top: 36px; }
  .article-back { display: inline-flex; align-items: center; min-height: 44px; color: var(--color-accent); font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 22px; }
  .article-heading h1 { font-size: clamp(34px, 5vw, 54px); line-height: 1.15; margin-top: 16px; letter-spacing: -.035em; }
  .article-deck { font-size: 20px; line-height: 1.7; color: var(--color-muted); margin-top: 24px; }
  .article-byline { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 8px; font-size: 14px; color: var(--color-muted); padding-block: 22px 28px; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-line); }
  .article-body { font-size: 17px; line-height: 1.9; padding-top: 32px; overflow-wrap: break-word; }
  .article-body p + p { margin-top: 20px; }
  .article-body h2 { font-size: clamp(26px, 4vw, 32px); line-height: 1.35; margin-block: 48px 20px; letter-spacing: -.025em; }
  .article-body h3 { font-size: 22px; margin-block: 30px 14px; }
  .article-body ul, .article-body ol { padding-inline-start: 24px; margin-block: 20px; }
  .article-body ul { list-style: disc; }
  .article-body ol { list-style: decimal; }
  .article-body li + li { margin-top: 12px; }
  .article-body a:not(.text-link) { color: var(--color-accent); text-decoration: underline; text-underline-offset: 3px; }
  .article-body code { font-size: .9em; padding: 2px 5px; background: #edf1f7; border-radius: 3px; }
  .article-figure { margin-block: 30px 34px; }
  .article-figure img { display: block; width: 100%; height: auto; border: 1px solid var(--color-line); border-radius: 8px; background: #0d4d82; }
  .article-figure figcaption { margin-top: 10px; color: var(--color-muted); font-size: 13px; line-height: 1.6; }
  .repository-link { overflow-wrap: anywhere; }
  .article-contents { margin-block: 32px; padding: 24px; border: 1px solid var(--color-line); border-radius: 8px; background: white; font-size: 15px; }
  .article-contents > p { font-weight: 600; }
  .article-contents ol { margin-bottom: 0; }
  @media (max-width: 767px) {
    .article-page { padding-block: 24px 48px; }
    .article-heading { padding-top: 24px; }
    .article-deck { font-size: 18px; }
    .article-body { font-size: 16px; }
    .article-figure { margin-block: 24px 28px; }
    .article-contents { padding: 20px; }
  }
</style>
